          "unit": 3,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The smallest useful model is `For C_x H_y, ideal complete oxidation requires x + y/4 moles of O2 per mole fuel.`. Write it first in symbols because symbols expose which quantities are inputs and which are derived. Conduct a dimensional check. For example, an energy rate must have dimensions of power, and a dimensionless efficiency must not quietly carry a pressure unit. Then inspect the limiting cases. A vanishing input, a zero flow or an impossible temperature ratio should trigger a validity warning rather than a numerically polished answer. The equation belongs to the assumptions stated with it, not to every engine.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** Methane CH4 requires two O2 molecules per fuel molecule and produces one CO2 plus two H2O.</p>",
          "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** Balance methane as CH4 + 2 O2 \u2192 CO2 + 2 H2O. Count carbon: one on each side. Count hydrogen: four on each side. Count oxygen: four atoms on each side. The oxygen coefficient follows x + y/4 = 1 + 4/4 = 2. To calculate air rather than pure oxygen, specify the oxygen fraction and mass basis of air.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Real exhaust contains additional species; air is not pure oxygen and mixture preparation is imperfect. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Conservation audit:** Start by marking the system boundary on a sketch. Separate fuel energy entering the cylinder from indicated work on the piston and brake work at the shaft. A computed improvement that violates energy conservation is a mistake, not a new engine phenomenon. Where an experimental result seems to contradict the calculation, investigate calibration, hidden storage, auxiliary loads and the interpretation of reference conditions before adding complexity to the model. Applied to **fuel composition and stoichiometric air demand**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
