          "unit": 3,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> Use `lambda=AFR_actual/AFR_stoich and phi=1/lambda for the same basis.` as the initial closure relation. It is tempting to interpret the number as a design guarantee; instead define a reference operating point, perturb each independent input separately and observe the output response. Check whether apparently independent variables are actually correlated by conservation or control logic. Treat a high-sensitivity variable as a candidate for better measurement. If the result requires a parameter measured by the same output that is being predicted, the model is partly circular and its validation requires an independent observation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** If stoichiometric AFR is 14.7 and actual AFR is 16.17, lambda=1.10.</p>",
          "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** The air\u2013fuel ratio comparison is 16.17/14.7 = 1.10, so \u03bb=1.10. The corresponding equivalence ratio is \u03c6=1/1.10 \u2248 0.909. A change in fuel chemical composition changes the stoichiometric reference, so it can change the calculated \u03bb even at an otherwise similar measured actual AFR.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Dry versus wet gas concentrations and sensor cross-sensitivity can complicate interpretation. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Scale and sensitivity:** Scaling a geometric or thermal parameter is not a free performance gain. With every scale change, ask which transport time, stress, temperature, pressure ratio or nondimensional group changes with it. If the underlying response is nonlinear, quote a local sensitivity around the stated operating point instead of applying a single proportional rule across an engine map. Applied to **air\u2013fuel ratio, lambda and equivalence ratio**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
