          "unit": 3,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The smallest useful model is `A characteristic burn time can be approximated as characteristic length / effective flame speed.`. Write it first in symbols because symbols expose which quantities are inputs and which are derived. Conduct a dimensional check. For example, an energy rate must have dimensions of power, and a dimensionless efficiency must not quietly carry a pressure unit. Then inspect the limiting cases. A vanishing input, a zero flow or an impossible temperature ratio should trigger a validity warning rather than a numerically polished answer. The equation belongs to the assumptions stated with it, not to every engine.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** A 50 mm travel distance at an assumed 10 m/s effective speed implies 5 ms; it is not a validated engine burn duration.</p>",
          "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** For the deliberately simple travel-distance model, 50 mm is 0.050 m. Dividing by 10 m/s yields 0.005 s = 5 ms. At 3000 rpm this spans 90 crank degrees, indicating how a crude travel model can overpredict meaningful burn intervals. Use this as a warning about effective flame speed, not a combustion calibration.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Laminar flame speed should not be substituted directly for a highly turbulent cylinder burn speed. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Experimental identifiability:** A neat equation does not imply that each parameter can be determined from available measurements. Two unknown effects may create the same measured output, especially when only torque and speed are logged. Design an independent measurement that distinguishes the competing explanations; when this is impossible, report a bounded family of plausible parameter values rather than a falsely precise single answer. Applied to **flame propagation and abnormal combustion**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
