          "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U05-L02",
          "unit": 5,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The working relation used here is `Injection quantity can be estimated from calibrated mass-flow rate integrated over the event.`. Define every variable before substitution, keep a single system of units, and check whether its result should increase or decrease when an input changes. Ask which assumptions make the expression possible: steady conditions, a perfect gas, constant properties, a lumped state, negligible pressure loss or an ideal mechanical linkage may be necessary. If an assumption does not hold in the intended application, identify the direction of the expected deviation when physics permits. When that direction is not clear, do not guess; schedule a measurement or use a higher-fidelity calculation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** A hypothetical 10 mg/ms steady injection rate over 3 ms gives 30 mg, ignoring opening and closing transients.</p>",
          "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** At a hypothetical injector rate of 10 mg/ms, a rectangular 3 ms pulse would inject 30 mg. Real opening and closing transitions reduce the useful effective interval, and rail pressure changes flow. Instead of treating the rectangle as a ground-truth injection event, integrate a measured injector flow trace when practical.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Multiple pulses and rail-pressure dynamics make square-pulse approximations unreliable. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Scale and sensitivity:** Scaling a geometric or thermal parameter is not a free performance gain. With every scale change, ask which transport time, stress, temperature, pressure ratio or nondimensional group changes with it. If the underlying response is nonlinear, quote a local sensitivity around the stated operating point instead of applying a single proportional rule across an engine map. Applied to **compression-ignition injection and mixing**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
