          "unit": 5,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> Use `Simple integral controller u[k+1]=u[k]+K_i*(lambda_target-lambda_measured).` as the initial closure relation. It is tempting to interpret the number as a design guarantee; instead define a reference operating point, perturb each independent input separately and observe the output response. Check whether apparently independent variables are actually correlated by conservation or control logic. Treat a high-sensitivity variable as a candidate for better measurement. If the result requires a parameter measured by the same output that is being predicted, the model is partly circular and its validation requires an independent observation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** If measured lambda is 1.03 while target is 1.00, the calibration must correct in the physically appropriate direction.</p>",
          "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** At \u03bb_measured=1.03, the exhaust indication is slightly leaner than \u03bb_target=1.00, assuming the sensor and reference are valid. Correcting toward stoichiometry normally requires increasing delivered fuel relative to air. A feedback sign that instead reduces fuel will push \u03bb farther above one and can amplify a control problem.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Incorrect feedback sign or long delay can produce sustained oscillation. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Experimental identifiability:** A neat equation does not imply that each parameter can be determined from available measurements. Two unknown effects may create the same measured output, especially when only torque and speed are logged. Design an independent measurement that distinguishes the competing explanations; when this is impossible, report a bounded family of plausible parameter values rather than a falsely precise single answer. Applied to **oxygen sensing and closed-loop mixture control**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
