          "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U05-L05",
          "unit": 5,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The working relation used here is `A first-order transient model is x_dot=(u-x)/tau; this is a simplification, not a full fuel-film law.`. Define every variable before substitution, keep a single system of units, and check whether its result should increase or decrease when an input changes. Ask which assumptions make the expression possible: steady conditions, a perfect gas, constant properties, a lumped state, negligible pressure loss or an ideal mechanical linkage may be necessary. If an assumption does not hold in the intended application, identify the direction of the expected deviation when physics permits. When that direction is not clear, do not guess; schedule a measurement or use a higher-fidelity calculation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** For tau=0.2 s a 0.1 s change reaches about 39% of its step under the linear model.</p>",
