        {
          "id": "prob-internal-combustion-engines-8-1",
          "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U08-L01",
          "unit": 8,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> Use `A consistent energy comparison is chemical input = fuel mass * specified lower heating value.` as the initial closure relation. It is tempting to interpret the number as a design guarantee; instead define a reference operating point, perturb each independent input separately and observe the output response. Check whether apparently independent variables are actually correlated by conservation or control logic. Treat a high-sensitivity variable as a candidate for better measurement. If the result requires a parameter measured by the same output that is being predicted, the model is partly circular and its validation requires an independent observation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** At 0.010 kg of fuel with assumed LHV 120 MJ/kg, input is 1.2 MJ before conversion losses.</p>",
          "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** With an assumed lower heating value of 120 MJ/kg and fuel mass 0.010 kg, the chemical input is 120\u00d70.010 = 1.2 MJ. This is not the amount of brake work: conversion efficiency and storage auxiliaries remain. The assumed LHV should never be interpreted as a property value that applies to all fuel blends or storage conditions.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Hydrogen operation can still form thermal NOx in air and requires stringent leak management. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Conservation audit:** Start by marking the system boundary on a sketch. Separate fuel energy entering the cylinder from indicated work on the piston and brake work at the shaft. A computed improvement that violates energy conservation is a mistake, not a new engine phenomenon. Where an experimental result seems to contradict the calculation, investigate calibration, hidden storage, auxiliary loads and the interpretation of reference conditions before adding complexity to the model. Applied to **hydrogen and oxygenated-fuel engine fundamentals**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
        },
        {
          "id": "prob-internal-combustion-engines-8-2",
          "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U08-L02",
          "unit": 8,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The working relation used here is `Total battery energy change = integral electrical power into battery dt with specified sign.`. Define every variable before substitution, keep a single system of units, and check whether its result should increase or decrease when an input changes. Ask which assumptions make the expression possible: steady conditions, a perfect gas, constant properties, a lumped state, negligible pressure loss or an ideal mechanical linkage may be necessary. If an assumption does not hold in the intended application, identify the direction of the expected deviation when physics permits. When that direction is not clear, do not guess; schedule a measurement or use a higher-fidelity calculation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** If a pack supplies 5 kW for 0.2 h ideally, delivered electrical energy is 1 kWh.</p>",
          "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** A battery delivering 5 kW for 0.2 hour transfers 5\u00d70.2 = 1 kWh before considering electric conversion and thermal losses. If a hybrid drive begins at high state of charge and ends substantially lower, attributing all delivered shaft work to fuel alone would misstate the comparison. Use an energy-equivalent state-of-charge correction.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Charge-sustaining comparisons require end-state-of-charge correction and route controls. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Scale and sensitivity:** Scaling a geometric or thermal parameter is not a free performance gain. With every scale change, ask which transport time, stress, temperature, pressure ratio or nondimensional group changes with it. If the underlying response is nonlinear, quote a local sensitivity around the stated operating point instead of applying a single proportional rule across an engine map. Applied to **hybrid powertrains and engine operating points**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
        },
        {
          "id": "prob-internal-combustion-engines-8-3",
          "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U08-L03",
          "unit": 8,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The smallest useful model is `CHP utilization = (useful electric output + useful recovered heat)/fuel input on a common energy basis.`. Write it first in symbols because symbols expose which quantities are inputs and which are derived. Conduct a dimensional check. For example, an energy rate must have dimensions of power, and a dimensionless efficiency must not quietly carry a pressure unit. Then inspect the limiting cases. A vanishing input, a zero flow or an impossible temperature ratio should trigger a validity warning rather than a numerically polished answer. The equation belongs to the assumptions stated with it, not to every engine.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** For 100 kW fuel input, 32 kW electric and 40 kW useful heat, utilization is 72%.</p>",
          "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** For fuel input 100 kW, useful electric output 32 kW and useful heat delivered 40 kW, combined utilization is (32+40)/100 = 72%. The uncounted 28 kW includes losses in this simplified boundary. Heat dumped to the environment is not useful heat, regardless of whether a thermal exchanger could theoretically intercept it.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Counting heat with no useful demand inflates performance; temperature quality matters. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Time dependence:** Engines operate through rapid events and slowly changing component states simultaneously. A crank-angle snapshot can describe the valve position but not warm-up; an averaged steady flow can describe laboratory demand but hide combustion variability. State the time scale to which each measured quantity belongs before comparing it with a model prediction. Applied to **waste-heat recovery and combined heat and power**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
        },
        {
          "id": "prob-internal-combustion-engines-8-4",
          "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U08-L04",
          "unit": 8,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> Use `Annual fuel cost = annual chemical energy demand / average efficiency * fuel price per energy unit.` as the initial closure relation. It is tempting to interpret the number as a design guarantee; instead define a reference operating point, perturb each independent input separately and observe the output response. Check whether apparently independent variables are actually correlated by conservation or control logic. Treat a high-sensitivity variable as a candidate for better measurement. If the result requires a parameter measured by the same output that is being predicted, the model is partly circular and its validation requires an independent observation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** A 100 MWh/year shaft demand at 30% average brake efficiency requires about 333 MWh fuel energy.</p>",
          "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** A yearly shaft energy requirement of 100 MWh at average brake efficiency 0.30 implies chemical fuel energy = 100/0.30 = 333.3 MWh. A peak-efficiency estimate of 0.40 would instead imply 250 MWh and understate expected consumption if typical operation is inefficient. Always apply a duty-weighted, physically justified efficiency.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Start/stop, ambient temperature and degradation make a single map-point extrapolation weak. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Experimental identifiability:** A neat equation does not imply that each parameter can be determined from available measurements. Two unknown effects may create the same measured output, especially when only torque and speed are logged. Design an independent measurement that distinguishes the competing explanations; when this is impossible, report a bounded family of plausible parameter values rather than a falsely precise single answer. Applied to **engine selection under lifecycle and duty constraints**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
        },
        {
          "id": "prob-internal-combustion-engines-8-5",
          "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U08-L05",
          "unit": 8,
          "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The working relation used here is `A simple weighted score S=sum(w_i*s_i) is a decision aid only when weights and units are explicit.`. Define every variable before substitution, keep a single system of units, and check whether its result should increase or decrease when an input changes. Ask which assumptions make the expression possible: steady conditions, a perfect gas, constant properties, a lumped state, negligible pressure loss or an ideal mechanical linkage may be necessary. If an assumption does not hold in the intended application, identify the direction of the expected deviation when physics permits. When that direction is not clear, do not guess; schedule a measurement or use a higher-fidelity calculation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** Weights 0.4 economy, 0.35 durability and 0.25 emissions must sum to one before computing a composite.</p>",
          "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** For weights 0.40, 0.35 and 0.25, verify 0.40+0.35+0.25=1.00 before scoring. An alternative may score highly while breaching an emissions or machinery-safety limit; any mandatory limit must be tested as a pass/fail condition separately. A weighted score is then a secondary aid to compare only feasible candidate solutions.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Weights can conceal hard constraints such as safety or legal limits that cannot be traded away. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Operating-envelope reasoning:** Any model can look successful at its calibration point. The meaningful question is whether its error stays acceptably small at cold start, hot idle, high load, low ambient pressure, and during rapid change. Define this intended envelope explicitly, keep data from some points for validation, and reject extrapolation outside known physical or measurement limits. Applied to **integrated calibration, verification and design review**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
        }
