      "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U01-L02",
      "unit": 1,
      "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The smallest useful model is `V_d = (pi/4) * B^2 * S * n_c; all linear dimensions in meters.`. Write it first in symbols because symbols expose which quantities are inputs and which are derived. Conduct a dimensional check. For example, an energy rate must have dimensions of power, and a dimensionless efficiency must not quietly carry a pressure unit. Then inspect the limiting cases. A vanishing input, a zero flow or an impossible temperature ratio should trigger a validity warning rather than a numerically polished answer. The equation belongs to the assumptions stated with it, not to every engine.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** Four cylinders at bore 0.086 m and stroke 0.086 m displace approximately 2.0 L in total.</p>",
      "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** Convert the 86 mm dimensions to 0.086 m before cubing. One cylinder displaces (\u03c0/4)(0.086)\u00b2(0.086) = 0.0004996 m\u00b3. Multiplying by four cylinders yields 0.001998 m\u00b3, or 1.998 L. The bore and stroke are both rounded, so do not present the displacement as a precision metrology result.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** A catalog nominal displacement may be rounded and does not state the clearance volume. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Scale and sensitivity:** Scaling a geometric or thermal parameter is not a free performance gain. With every scale change, ask which transport time, stress, temperature, pressure ratio or nondimensional group changes with it. If the underlying response is nonlinear, quote a local sensitivity around the stated operating point instead of applying a single proportional rule across an engine map. Applied to **bore, stroke, displacement and chamber volume**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
