      "id": "prob-internal-combustion-engines-1-4",
      "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U01-L04",
      "unit": 1,
      "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The working relation used here is `U_mean = 2*S*N/60; N in revolutions per minute, S in meters.`. Define every variable before substitution, keep a single system of units, and check whether its result should increase or decrease when an input changes. Ask which assumptions make the expression possible: steady conditions, a perfect gas, constant properties, a lumped state, negligible pressure loss or an ideal mechanical linkage may be necessary. If an assumption does not hold in the intended application, identify the direction of the expected deviation when physics permits. When that direction is not clear, do not guess; schedule a measurement or use a higher-fidelity calculation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** At 6000 rpm and stroke 0.09 m, mean piston speed is 18 m/s.</p>",
      "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** The piston traverses one stroke upward and one stroke downward per crank revolution. Its total path per revolution is therefore 2(0.09) = 0.18 m. At 6000 rpm, 0.18\u00d76000/60 = 18 m/s mean piston speed. This does not claim that the instantaneous speed is constant over any crank revolution.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Mean speed is not peak speed or ring sliding speed at every crank angle. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Experimental identifiability:** A neat equation does not imply that each parameter can be determined from available measurements. Two unknown effects may create the same measured output, especially when only torque and speed are logged. Design an independent measurement that distinguishes the competing explanations; when this is impossible, report a bounded family of plausible parameter values rather than a falsely precise single answer. Applied to **crankshaft kinematics and piston speed**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
