      "id": "prob-internal-combustion-engines-1-5",
      "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U01-L05",
      "unit": 1,
      "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The smallest useful model is `P_b = 2*pi*(N/60)*T; convert W to kW by dividing 1000.`. Write it first in symbols because symbols expose which quantities are inputs and which are derived. Conduct a dimensional check. For example, an energy rate must have dimensions of power, and a dimensionless efficiency must not quietly carry a pressure unit. Then inspect the limiting cases. A vanishing input, a zero flow or an impossible temperature ratio should trigger a validity warning rather than a numerically polished answer. The equation belongs to the assumptions stated with it, not to every engine.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** A shaft producing 200 N m at 3000 rpm delivers about 62.8 kW.</p>",
      "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** Convert 3000 rpm to 50 revolutions per second. The shaft angular velocity is 2\u03c0\u00d750 = 314.159 rad/s. Multiply by measured torque 200 N\u00b7m to get 62,831.9 W or 62.83 kW. At a different speed, identical torque produces different shaft power; this is why a dynamometer map needs both axes.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** A single peak power figure omits fuel consumption, thermal limits and speed range. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Operating-envelope reasoning:** Any model can look successful at its calibration point. The meaningful question is whether its error stays acceptably small at cold start, hot idle, high load, low ambient pressure, and during rapid change. Define this intended envelope explicitly, keep data from some points for validation, and reject extrapolation outside known physical or measurement limits. Applied to **torque, power and normalized engine maps**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
