      "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U02-L02",
      "unit": 2,
      "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The working relation used here is `eta_Otto = 1 - r_c^(1-gamma) for constant gamma and ideal gas.`. Define every variable before substitution, keep a single system of units, and check whether its result should increase or decrease when an input changes. Ask which assumptions make the expression possible: steady conditions, a perfect gas, constant properties, a lumped state, negligible pressure loss or an ideal mechanical linkage may be necessary. If an assumption does not hold in the intended application, identify the direction of the expected deviation when physics permits. When that direction is not clear, do not guess; schedule a measurement or use a higher-fidelity calculation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** For r_c=10 and gamma=1.4, eta is about 60.2% in the air-standard model.</p>",
      "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** For compression ratio r=10 and \u03b3=1.4, use \u03b7 = 1 \u2212 1/10^0.4. Because 10^0.4 \u2248 2.5119, \u03b7 \u2248 1 \u2212 0.3981 = 0.6019 or 60.19%. The result is an ideal air-standard sensitivity estimate; it cannot be read as a measured brake thermal efficiency.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Actual brake efficiency is lower because combustion, heat transfer, pumping and friction are omitted. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Scale and sensitivity:** Scaling a geometric or thermal parameter is not a free performance gain. With every scale change, ask which transport time, stress, temperature, pressure ratio or nondimensional group changes with it. If the underlying response is nonlinear, quote a local sensitivity around the stated operating point instead of applying a single proportional rule across an engine map. Applied to **otto-cycle compression and expansion**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
