      "unit": 2,
      "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The smallest useful model is `eta_Diesel=1-(1/r_c^(gamma-1))*((rho^gamma-1)/(gamma*(rho-1))).`. Write it first in symbols because symbols expose which quantities are inputs and which are derived. Conduct a dimensional check. For example, an energy rate must have dimensions of power, and a dimensionless efficiency must not quietly carry a pressure unit. Then inspect the limiting cases. A vanishing input, a zero flow or an impossible temperature ratio should trigger a validity warning rather than a numerically polished answer. The equation belongs to the assumptions stated with it, not to every engine.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** At fixed compression ratio, a larger ideal cutoff ratio generally reduces efficiency in this simple model.</p>",
      "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** Consider the air-standard relation at r=17, \u03b3=1.4 and cutoff \u03c1=2. The compression factor is 17^0.4 \u2248 3.106, while the heat-addition factor is (2^1.4\u22121)/(1.4\u00d7(2\u22121)) \u2248 1.1708. Hence \u03b7 \u2248 1 \u2212 1.1708/3.106 \u2248 0.623. Compare another cutoff at the same compression ratio rather than changing two assumptions at once.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Real compression-ignition heat release is not constant-pressure and is shaped by mixing. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Time dependence:** Engines operate through rapid events and slowly changing component states simultaneously. A crank-angle snapshot can describe the valve position but not warm-up; an averaged steady flow can describe laboratory demand but hide combustion variability. State the time scale to which each measured quantity belongs before comparing it with a model prediction. Applied to **diesel-cycle cutoff ratio and heat addition**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
