      "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** Set hot-side inlet 400 K, outlet 310 K and cooling-medium inlet 300 K. Effectiveness is (400\u2212310)/(400\u2212300) = 90/100 = 0.90. A temperature approach of 10 K looks attractive, but cooling pressure loss and thermal storage still influence net engine operation. The same temperature ratio alone cannot supply an overall effectiveness map.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** A large apparent effectiveness may hide pressure drop and inadequate transient response. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Operating-envelope reasoning:** Any model can look successful at its calibration point. The meaningful question is whether its error stays acceptably small at cold start, hot idle, high load, low ambient pressure, and during rapid change. Define this intended envelope explicitly, keep data from some points for validation, and reject extrapolation outside known physical or measurement limits. Applied to **intercooling, exhaust backpressure and system matching**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
