      "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The working relation used here is `FMEP = IMEP - BMEP with a consistent four-stroke-cycle definition.`. Define every variable before substitution, keep a single system of units, and check whether its result should increase or decrease when an input changes. Ask which assumptions make the expression possible: steady conditions, a perfect gas, constant properties, a lumped state, negligible pressure loss or an ideal mechanical linkage may be necessary. If an assumption does not hold in the intended application, identify the direction of the expected deviation when physics permits. When that direction is not clear, do not guess; schedule a measurement or use a higher-fidelity calculation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** If IMEP=12 bar and BMEP=9.5 bar, FMEP is 2.5 bar.</p>",
      "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** With IMEP=12 bar and BMEP=9.5 bar on matching cycles and displacement, the estimated friction mean effective pressure is 2.5 bar. If the pressure analysis and shaft test refer to different load conditions, subtracting them would be meaningless. Also specify whether accessory loads are included in the brake measurement.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Apparent FMEP also absorbs model error in indicated pressure analysis and accessory definitions. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Time dependence:** Engines operate through rapid events and slowly changing component states simultaneously. A crank-angle snapshot can describe the valve position but not warm-up; an averaged steady flow can describe laboratory demand but hide combustion variability. State the time scale to which each measured quantity belongs before comparing it with a model prediction. Applied to **mechanical friction and friction mean effective pressure**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
