      "id": "prob-internal-combustion-engines-8-5",
      "title": "Calculation & Verification: Internal Combustion Engines \u2014 U08-L05",
      "unit": 8,
      "question": "<p><strong>Problem Statement:</strong> The working relation used here is `A simple weighted score S=sum(w_i*s_i) is a decision aid only when weights and units are explicit.`. Define every variable before substitution, keep a single system of units, and check whether its result should increase or decrease when an input changes. Ask which assumptions make the expression possible: steady conditions, a perfect gas, constant properties, a lumped state, negligible pressure loss or an ideal mechanical linkage may be necessary. If an assumption does not hold in the intended application, identify the direction of the expected deviation when physics permits. When that direction is not clear, do not guess; schedule a measurement or use a higher-fidelity calculation.\n\n**Assumed numerical case:** Weights 0.4 economy, 0.35 durability and 0.25 emissions must sum to one before computing a composite.</p>",
      "solution": "<div class=\"step-explanation\"><p><strong>Worked Engineering Derivation:</strong></p><p>**Calculation walkthrough:** For weights 0.40, 0.35 and 0.25, verify 0.40+0.35+0.25=1.00 before scoring. An alternative may score highly while breaching an emissions or machinery-safety limit; any mandatory limit must be tested as a pass/fail condition separately. A weighted score is then a secondary aid to compare only feasible candidate solutions.\n\nThe stated values form a hypothetical teaching example and should be recalculated by hand. First write the governing relation, then translate the inputs to SI, then perform substitution before rounding the displayed output. Check the sign and order of magnitude against a physical limiting case. Finally, ask which measured input would dominate an uncertainty interval. A large discrepancy from an independently computed check is grounds to revise the work, not to adjust the accepted answer without explanation.\n\n### 3. Interpret the result, including failures\n\n**Critical interpretation:** Weights can conceal hard constraints such as safety or legal limits that cannot be traded away. A misleading numerical result can arise even when the arithmetic is perfect: the problem may be a wrong system boundary, an inappropriate reference property, a time-averaged signal used for a crank-angle event, or an unrecorded controller response. State which of those failure mechanisms is most likely here. Think about the change that would be observed in cylinder pressure, exhaust temperature, manifold state, or brake output if the explanatory mechanism were genuinely active rather than merely correlated with an unrelated change.\n\n**Operating-envelope reasoning:** Any model can look successful at its calibration point. The meaningful question is whether its error stays acceptably small at cold start, hot idle, high load, low ambient pressure, and during rapid change. Define this intended envelope explicitly, keep data from some points for validation, and reject extrapolation outside known physical or measurement limits. Applied to **integrated calibration, verification and design review**, this discipline helps decide whether the model is a prediction with independent evidence or merely a restatement of its input measurements. Write the most restrictive assumption in the margin of the calculation, then revise the interpretation if that assumption fails under the case-study operating conditions.</p></div>"
